    {
      id: "w7-l5",
      title: "Build the messages with AI",
      minutes: 4,
      body: "Four emotions times three groups is twelve messages. Nobody writes twelve good messages from scratch in a week while running two call sessions a day. So you don't. You get Claude or ChatGPT to do the first draft, then you rework it until it sounds like you.\n\n## The prompt\n\nThe full prompt is in the AI message prompt template. It tells the tool who you are and where you work, and which group the lead is in. It asks for four messages, one per emotion, each as four talking points for a short video plus an email of 120 words or fewer with a subject line. It sets the give-or-ask rule for that group, rules out invented statistics, leaves a [bracket] wherever you have not supplied a number, and asks for short, plain Australian English with no sales pitch.\n\nFill the brackets before you run it. Under ABOUT ME, a new associate writes their name, Ray White Mildura, the suburbs in their farm, and three office results the listing agent has cleared, described as \"our office sold\". Under THE LEAD, run it once for a cold lead, once for a warm lead and once for a hot lead. Three runs, twelve drafts.\n\n## Then ask for these\n\nThe template lists four follow-up requests:\n\n- Give me this as four video bullet points.\n- Cut the email down and make it warmer.\n- Turn it into an SMS of 300 characters or fewer.\n- Work in my figures: [recent sales, median price, open home attendance].\n\nThe second one is the one you will use most. First drafts are nearly always too long and too polite. Ask for shorter and warmer, twice if you need to, and it starts to read like a person wrote it.\n\n## Three rules\n\n- AI writes the draft. You read it aloud before it goes anywhere.\n- Every number gets checked against your own figures before it goes out.\n- Sent beats perfect. Send the first one today.\n\nReading aloud is not optional. If a line makes you cringe when you say it, a cold lead will cringe reading it. Change it to what you would really say on the phone. The numbers rule is the one that protects you. Left alone, an AI will happily make up a median price for Irymple. Every figure comes from office data, or it stays in brackets.\n\n## Office guidance: what never goes in the prompt\n\nDescribe the lead by group only. Never paste a lead's name, address, phone number, email or anything else personal into the tool. The AI does not need to know it is Josh in Red Cliffs to write a decent market update, and our privacy obligations still apply however clever the tool is.\n\n## What done looks like on Friday\n\nTwelve drafts. Each one read aloud to a buddy, Damian or Luke. Each number checked. All twelve saved in the CRM as templates named by group and emotion: Cold, Proof. Warm, Fears. And so on. Next week they go onto days 1 to 28 of the three 30-day trust plans, and the first one gets filmed.",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "Three runs of the prompt, one per group, give you twelve drafts: four emotions by three groups.",
        "Read every draft aloud, check every number against office data, and get the first one out.",
        "No personal lead details go into the prompt. Describe the lead by group only."
      ],
      keyLine: "Let the AI do the typing. Make sure it still sounds like you."
    },
    {
      id: "w7-l6",
      title: "Your next 30 days of leads: the maths",
      minutes: 4,
      body: "You finish the week with one question.\n\n> How many people could realistically list with you in the next 30 days? Count them by name. Then sort them into cold, warm and hot.\n\n## The 30-day lead count\n\n- Listing leads for the next 30 days, total: [number]\n- Hot. As good as yours: [number]\n- Warm. They know you, but will still talk to other agents: [number]\n- Cold. No trust yet: [number]\n- Share of the total that is warm or cold: [per cent]\n- The office's average commission per listing sold: [dollars]\n- Value of converting half the warm and cold leads: [dollars]\n\nTwo of these lines need explaining.\n\nOffice guidance: a listing lead is not every contact. It is someone who, from what they have told you, could list in the next 30 days. That might be an appraisal in the soonest window on your pipeline (1 to 2 months), a hot lead who has named a date, or a cold lead from an open home who said they will sell before they buy. On the guide you have around 17 appraisals behind you, and only some are live for the next 30 days. Count honestly. Three is a real number. So is zero, and it tells you what next week's plans are for.\n\nThe average commission per listing sold is an office figure. Ask Damian or Luke rather than guessing. The database maths in Database Foundations works on $10,000 a sale as a round figure. For this sheet, use the office's real number.\n\n## A worked example, not a target\n\nSay you count 8 listing leads: 1 hot, 3 warm and 4 cold. Warm and cold are 7 of the 8, nearly 90 per cent. Convert half of those 7, call it 3, at the round figure of $10,000 a sale, and the last line reads $30,000. That is what principles 1 to 3, done properly for 30 days, are worth on a pipeline of eight. Your 3 listings for the program are sitting in that line.\n\nNow go back to the per cent line. Principle 1 said most of your leads sit in cold and warm, and other agents are chasing them too. This sheet puts a dollar figure on winning them.\n\n## What goes in the app\n\nPut the three counts in your one-line note on the Today tab. They come straight from your principle 1 tags. Bring the sheet to Monday's meeting with the dollar line filled in. Then open the 30-Day Commitment and fill in lines 1 and 2 with these numbers. The other seven lines get signed next week.\n\n## Going into week 8\n\nBy Friday you should have every contact tagged, four gaps with due dates, twelve messages saved in the CRM, three partners who said yes to 10 per cent, and a named count of your 30-day leads. Re-score the Self-Check on Friday. Items 1, 2, 3 and 8 should now be ticks. Item 9 follows next week, once the three plans are loaded. Week 8 is the build: three 30-day trust plans loaded in the CRM, your first one-to-one market update filmed, and your first listing expected. Seven weeks of prospecting got you the appraisals. The next 30 days of trust turn them into listings.",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "Write down your listing leads for the next 30 days, by name, and split them into hot, warm and cold.",
        "The sheet values converting half of the warm and cold leads at the office's average commission. That is where your 3 listings sit.",
        "Zero is an honest answer, and it tells you what next week's 30-day trust plans are for."
      ],
      keyLine: "Count your next 30 days of leads by name. Then sort them cold, warm and hot."
    }
