// Launchpad 300.30.3 - TEMPLATES library (letters, emails, SMS, forms, checklists, worksheets).
// Ray White Mildura's own templates. Office guidance is labelled as such in each body.

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "street-appraisal-letter",
  "title": "Street Appraisal letter (street by street)",
  "kind": "letter",
  "when": "Post to every home on a farm street so it arrives 3 to 5 days before the two Street Appraisal Days you have booked for that street.",
  "body": "- Name: [owner name]\n- Address: [owner address]\n\nHi [first name],\n\nRe: [property address]\n\n**HOW HAVE [SUBURB] PRICES MOVED THIS YEAR?**\n\nValues in [suburb] shift over time. Most owners we talk with want to know where their home sits today, because it helps them plan their next financial step.\n\nSo we are offering every home in this area a free market appraisal.\n\nIt takes about 15 minutes, and we are working through the area street by street.\n\n**WHAT YOU GET**\n\n- A letter setting out the appraised value of your home\n- A market analysis of homes sold nearby in the last six months\n\n**HOW TO TAKE PART**\n\nCall [your name] at Ray White Mildura on [mobile], or email [email].\n\n**WHEN WE WILL BE IN YOUR STREET**\n\nWe have set aside [day] and [day] for your street. If neither day suits, let me know and we will find another time.\n\nEverything you tell us stays confidential. I look forward to meeting you.\n\nKind regards,\n[your name]\nRay White Mildura, [mobile]\n\nNote: If your property is currently listed with another agent under an exclusive agency agreement, please disregard this letter.\n\n## Before you send\n\n- The headline asks how [suburb] prices moved this year, so it never claims more than the sales show. If recent sales clearly show values rising, you may use \"[SUBURB] VALUES ARE RISING\" instead. Never claim a rise you cannot show.\n- The letter promises two things: a letter of appraised value and a six-month sales analysis. Have both ready for every home you appraise. Ask Damian or Luke for the office format.\n- Merge one street at a time, 3 to 5 days before its two Street Appraisal Days, from the street calendar you built in week 2.\n- Every owner who calls or emails back is a booked Doorstep Appraisal. Offer two times: \"Does 2pm or 4pm suit?\"\n- Every door on the street is geo data, whether they want the appraisal or not. Name, phone or email, and address go into the CRM that night."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "seven-year-letter",
  "title": "Seven-year letter (settlement anniversary)",
  "kind": "letter",
  "when": "Send in the first week of each month to every farm owner who settled seven years ago that month, then call the same owners a week later.",
  "body": "- Date: [date]\n- Name: [owner name]\n- Address: [address]\n- Suburb: [suburb]\n- Settlement date: [settlement date]\n\nDear [first name],\n\n**SEVEN YEARS IN YOUR HOME. HAPPY ANNIVERSARY!**\n\nIt is seven years this month since you settled on your home. I hope they have been good years, with plenty more good ones ahead. Property values move with time too, and you may be surprised by what your home is worth today.\n\nAs a thank-you to local owners, we would like to offer you a free appraisal. It is useful if you are thinking about selling. It is just as useful if you simply want a figure for insurance, or want to know how much equity you have built up and what that could mean for a move.\n\nTo book yours, call [your name] on [mobile] or email [email].\n\nYours sincerely,\n[your name]\nRay White Mildura\n\nNote: If your property is currently listed with another agent under an exclusive agency agreement, please disregard this letter.\n\n## Before you send\n\n- This letter only works if the settlement date is in the CRM. Capture it with every piece of geo data where you can, even roughly (\"we bought in 2019\").\n- Build a smart list in the CRM: settled seven years ago this month. Run it in the first week of every month.\n- The letter opens the door. The call a week later books the appraisal. Use the database call script, point its Received beat at the letter, and offer two times.\n- No settlement dates yet? Ask Damian or Luke what sales history the office holds for your farm streets."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "twelve-month-appraisal-letter",
  "title": "12-month appraisal letter (office voice)",
  "kind": "letter",
  "when": "Send 12 months after any appraisal when you want the reminder in the office's voice rather than your own; use this or the 12-month re-appraisal letter, never both.",
  "body": "- Date: [date]\n- Name: [owner name]\n- Address: [address]\n\nDear [first name],\n\nA quick note to let you know it is now 12 months since we gave you a free appraisal on your home at [address].\n\nAt Ray White Mildura we keep in touch with the owners we meet. We want to look after your interests right up to the day you decide the time is right to sell.\n\nA year is a long time in property. Now is a good time for an updated appraisal of what your home would be worth on today's market.\n\nCall [your name] at the office on [mobile] and we will book a time that suits you.\n\nWe look forward to helping you, now and whenever you need us.\n\nRegards,\nRay White Mildura\n[your name], [mobile]\n\nNote: If your property is currently listed with another agent under an exclusive agency agreement, please disregard this letter.\n\n## Before you send\n\n- We write \"appraisal\", never \"valuation\". Only a licensed valuer gives a valuation.\n- Set the send date the day you log the appraisal: 12 months out. Send the anniversary SMS the same week.\n- Two letters do this job: this one and the 12-month re-appraisal letter. Pick one per contact. This one is shorter and in the office's voice; the other is in yours.\n- Every reply is a fresh Doorstep Appraisal and restarts the 12-month clock."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "twelve-month-reappraisal-automation-letter",
  "title": "12-month re-appraisal letter (sends itself)",
  "kind": "letter",
  "when": "Load into the CRM the day you log an appraisal so it sends itself 12 months later; this is how next year's appraisals book themselves.",
  "body": "- Date: [date]\n- Name: [owner name]\n- Address: [address]\n- Suburb: [suburb]\n\nDear [first name],\n\nRe: [property address]\n\nIt is a year since I appraised your home. Where did that year go?\n\nI specialise in this area, and I re-appraise homes here regularly so owners always know where they stand. I would like to offer you the same.\n\nThere is no cost. I do it every year so you have a current market price for your home. There is no obligation, and you do not need to be thinking about selling.\n\nKeen to take this up? Ring me this week and we will lock in a time that suits us both.\n\nI look forward to catching up.\n\nWarm regards,\n[your name]\nRay White Mildura, [mobile]\n\nNote: If your property is currently listed with another agent under an exclusive agency agreement, please disregard this letter.\n\n## Before you send\n\n- Set the date the day you log the appraisal. Do not rely on remembering; this letter is the system.\n- The anniversary SMS goes the same week: \"reply YES and I'll drop by\".\n- The exclusive-agency note stays on this letter every time.\n- Thirty appraisals in this program means thirty of these letters landing next year. That is a second year of appraisals you do not have to prospect for."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "introduction-letter",
  "title": "Introduction letter (day 2)",
  "kind": "letter",
  "when": "Post two days after every appraisal or market-report visit to Cold and Warm leads; it is the first letter in their 30-day trust plan.",
  "body": "Dear [first name],\n\nThank you for your time the other day, and for giving me the chance to show you how I work. When the time comes for your move, I would love to help you start that next chapter.\n\nOne of the first things I learned in real estate is that a good sale starts long before the sign goes up. So over the next few weeks I will send you a few short pieces to help you get ready, and to help your sale go well when you decide the time is right.\n\nI work a little differently from most agents. Real estate is not just putting a price on your home and ringing every week to ask if you are ready to sell! My job is to prepare you properly, explain how the sale process works, and give you what you need to make the right decisions.\n\nThanks again. I am looking forward to helping you with this next step.\n\nIf you have any questions at all in the meantime, call me on [mobile].\n\nKind regards,\n[your name], [title]\nRay White Mildura\n\n## Before you send\n\n- Day 2 of the Cold and Warm trust plans. Hot leads have already booked, so they skip it.\n- Hand-address the envelope. A handwritten envelope gets opened.\n- Paragraph two promises more letters over the coming weeks. That promise is the letter schedule. Only send this letter if the rest of the schedule is loaded and ready.\n- Personalise all six letters in week 6 and have Damian or Luke sign each one off before the first one goes out."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "testimonial-letter",
  "title": "Testimonial letter (day 9)",
  "kind": "letter",
  "when": "Post on day 9 of the Cold plan with three to five testimonials and the clients' phone numbers enclosed; it is the proof letter.",
  "body": "Dear [first name],\n\nThe best way to judge any real estate agent is to ask the people they have already helped. What past clients say tells you more than anything we could say about ourselves.\n\nSo I have enclosed a few words from people who bought or sold with Ray White Mildura this year.\n\nI am proud of the work we do, and I hope the care we bring shows in their words. I have included their phone numbers too. Please feel free to ring any of them. They have agreed to take your call.\n\nWhen you are ready to sell, I would love to help. Until then, call me on [mobile] with any questions at all.\n\nKind regards,\n[your name], [title]\nRay White Mildura\n\n## Before you send\n\n- You are new, so the testimonials belong to the office. The letter says the clients bought or sold with Ray White Mildura. Keep that wording, and never suggest the sales were yours.\n- Only enclose a client's number if that client has agreed to take calls. \"They have agreed to take your call\" must be true.\n- Three to five testimonials, in the clients' own words, with the suburb and the year. Ask Damian or Luke which ones you may use.\n- This is the proof letter for Cold leads: people who do not trust you yet. Let past clients do the talking."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "presentation-letter",
  "title": "Presentation letter (day 14)",
  "kind": "letter",
  "when": "Post on day 14 of the Warm and Hot plans with the home presentation tips sheet and your preferred trades enclosed.",
  "body": "Dear [first name],\n\nIf you want the best possible price, how your home presents to buyers matters more than almost anything else.\n\nThe good news is that many owners do a great job of presenting their home during a sale, and that gives them every chance of a strong result.\n\nI have enclosed some practical tips on getting your home ready so it sells for the most it can when you go to market. Even if your home is already in top shape, you may find an idea or two worth trying.\n\nGood luck with the finishing touches. If you have any questions, call me any time on [mobile].\n\nKind regards,\n[your name], [title]\nRay White Mildura\n\n## Before you send\n\n- Enclose the office's presentation tips sheet. If there is not one, build a one-page version in week 6 and get it signed off.\n- Once your Preferred Partners list is filled in, add this line after paragraph three: \"To help, here are local trades we trust and use ourselves: [cleaner], [painter], [handyman], [builder], [gardener or landscaper] and [stylist]. Tell them I sent you.\"\n- Letters and emails work alongside your calls and visits. The letter sets up the call: about a week after it lands, your touch-point call asks whether it arrived and what they thought.\n- This is the education letter. Nothing in it asks for the listing."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "legal-letter",
  "title": "Legal letter (day 18)",
  "kind": "letter",
  "when": "Post on day 18 of the Hot plan with three local conveyancers or solicitors listed; it answers the worry about the paperwork.",
  "body": "Dear [first name],\n\nI hope all is well.\n\nSelling your home is a big step, and a few early decisions make the whole process run smoothly. One of the first is choosing who will look after the legal side for you. They prepare the documents buyers need to see before your home is marketed, and they handle everything from the signed contract through to settlement day.\n\nWe have been selling homes for local families for many years, so we know the conveyancers and solicitors in this area well. Below are three we work with closely. If you do not already have someone, ask each of them for a quote, and tell them we sent you.\n\n**Conveyancers and solicitors**\n\n- [firm name], [contact person], [phone]\n- [firm name], [contact person], [phone]\n- [firm name], [contact person], [phone]\n\nIf you would rather we arranged it all for you, we are happy to. Any questions, call me on [mobile].\n\nKind regards,\n[your name], [title]\nRay White Mildura\n\n## Before you send\n\n- The three firms come from the office. Ask Damian or Luke who Ray White Mildura works with, and ring each contact person so they know your name.\n- Optional paragraph, if the office has a building and pest partner: \"We can also book a pre-sale building and pest inspection, so nothing catches you out when a buyer gets serious: [name, number].\"\n- Office guidance: in Victoria the vendor's conveyancer prepares the Section 32 vendor statement before the home can be marketed, so sending this letter early (day 18) saves weeks at launch.\n- Hot leads only. A Cold lead has not asked for help with paperwork yet."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "broker-letter",
  "title": "Broker letter (day 21)",
  "kind": "letter",
  "when": "Post on day 21 of the Hot plan, once the office's broker partner has agreed to call the vendor; it answers the worry about a sale falling over on finance.",
  "body": "Dear [first name],\n\nCould a mortgage broker help you get a higher price for your home?\n\nIt may sound like an odd question, but it is exactly why Ray White Mildura works with [broker], [one line about the broker].\n\nNot every lender will lend the same buyer the same amount, and the gap between them can be large. When you sell with us, every buyer for your home can talk with [broker]. That works in your favour twice. Buyers learn the most they can borrow across the whole market, so they can stretch further for your home. And because we check a buyer's finance before you accept their offer, there is far less risk of a sale subject to finance falling through.\n\nSomeone from [broker] will ring you to explain how they fit into the marketing of your home. You do not have to use them for your own loan. It is a short chat about how they help you get the best price.\n\nAny questions, please call us on [mobile].\n\nKind regards,\n[your name], [title]\nRay White Mildura\n\n## Before you send\n\n- Confirm the broker partner with Damian or Luke, and confirm the broker is happy to call every vendor you send this to. Do not promise a call that will not happen.\n- Replace \"[one line about the broker]\" with one true line about ours, such as their team or their lender panel. Nothing you cannot back up.\n- Keep the finance claims general. The broker explains their own service; you do not give lending advice.\n- If the broker pays the office a referral fee, say so plainly to the vendor and to any buyer you pass on, the same way the OFI callback does. Ask Damian or Luke how the office words it.\n- The Storage letter follows on day 24."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "storage-letter",
  "title": "Storage letter (day 24)",
  "kind": "letter",
  "when": "Post on day 24 of the Hot plan with the storage partner's client offer; it is the last piece of value before the day 25 call and the day 28 thank-you.",
  "body": "Dear [first name],\n\nAt Ray White Mildura we know how much time and care owners put into getting their home ready to sell.\n\nMore and more sellers now move some of their belongings into storage while their home is on the market. Less furniture means more open space, and buyers notice.\n\nWe have arranged a special rate with [storage company] that is only available to Ray White Mildura clients.\n\nTo use it, simply take this letter to [storage company] and their team will look after the rest.\n\nQuestions about the offer, or about anything property related? Call us on [mobile].\n\nKind regards,\n[your name], [title]\nRay White Mildura\n\n## Before you send\n\n- The offer has to exist. Ask Damian or Luke whether the office has a storage partner. If not, make it one of your three partner calls this week and aim for a 10% client discount.\n- Once you have a removalist, add: \"Moving as well? [removalist, number] is the removalist we recommend.\"\n- Hot leads only, day 24. The next touch is the day 25 call, where you ask whether any friends or neighbours are thinking of selling."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "just-listed-letter",
  "title": "Just Listed letter",
  "kind": "letter",
  "when": "Letterbox 50 to 100 homes around any new listing in or near your farm (yours, or an office listing you have been given) on the day it is listed.",
  "body": "Dear homeowner,\n\n**A home near you has just come on the market**\n\nRay White Mildura has just listed [address], not far from you.\n\nDo you know someone who would love to live in this neighbourhood?\n\nIf you would like details on the home, call me any time.\n\nMy card is enclosed. Keep it handy, or pass it on to anyone who might be interested.\n\nI will be in touch over the coming weeks to let you know how the campaign is going.\n\nYours sincerely,\n[your name]\nRay White Mildura, [mobile]\n\nNote: If your property is currently listed with another agent under an exclusive agency agreement, please disregard this letter.\n\n## Before you send\n\n- This is the first touch of the Just Listed campaign. Three days later you door knock and phone the same homes with an invitation to the neighbours' preview. The email and SMS go to database contacts nearby on the day you knock.\n- Working an office listing? Agree it with Damian or Luke and the listing agent first. The letter says \"Ray White Mildura has just listed\", which is true. Your card goes in because you are the one who will stay in touch.\n- Open a Campaign Snapshot for the property. Under When it lists, tick \"Letters in letterboxes\" and note the date and how many homes.\n- Every door you knock after this letter is a Fifty Neighbours conversation: the courtesy reason, the extra traffic, updating their details, and the cash-buyer question."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "just-sold-letter",
  "title": "Just Sold letter",
  "kind": "letter",
  "when": "Letterbox the same 50 to 100 homes the day a listing in or near your farm sells, then door knock and phone with the buyers-who-missed-out angle.",
  "body": "Dear homeowner,\n\n**JUST SOLD NEAR YOU, BY [AGENT NAME]**\n\nRay White Mildura has sold another home near you. This time it was [address].\n\nBuyer interest in [suburb] is strong right now, and once again several buyers missed out on this one. Thinking of selling, or know someone who is? Call [your name] at our office.\n\nWhen we take a home to market, we talk with buyers who want to live in this area. We have been part of this market for a long time, so buyers rely on us to know how it is tracking, and we work hard to give them better service than they expect.\n\nWant to know where your home sits in today's market? For a free, no-obligation appraisal, call me directly on [mobile] or drop into the office at [office address].\n\nKind regards,\n[your name]\nRay White Mildura\n\nNote: If your property is currently listed with another agent under an exclusive agency agreement, please disregard this letter.\n\n## Before you send\n\n- \"Several buyers missed out\" must be true. Check with the listing agent how many unsuccessful buyers there were before you write it.\n- The headline names the agent who sold the home. On an office sale, use the listing agent's name and sign the letter as yourself, or write \"JUST SOLD NEAR YOU, BY RAY WHITE MILDURA\".\n- The letter goes out the day the sale is agreed. The door knock and phone call follow within three days, while the board still says \"under contract\" or \"sold\".\n- Every YES at the door is an appraisal booking. Every NO still gets a warm close, in the script's words: \"No worries. If you ever need a hand with anything property-related, just give us a ring.\""
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "ofi-invitation",
  "title": "Open home invitation for neighbours",
  "kind": "letter",
  "when": "Letterbox the neighbours before any open home or neighbours' preview in or near your farm; every neighbour who comes is geo data and gets an OFI callback within 24 hours.",
  "body": "Dear homeowner,\n\nYou are invited to an open home in your neighbourhood.\n\n- Where: [address]\n- Day: [day]\n- Time: [time]\n\nWe are also happy to help with any other property question, whether you are thinking of buying, thinking of selling, or just keeping an eye on the market.\n\nTo find out how we can help, call me on [mobile].\n\nYours sincerely,\n[your name]\nRay White Mildura\n\n## Before you send\n\n- Use it for the neighbours' preview before launch (for example, Thursday at 5pm) and for the first public open.\n- Neighbours come to have a look. That is fine. Greet every one of them and take name, phone or email, and address. That is geo data plus a reason to call tomorrow.\n- The callback is the OFI callback: an appraisal opportunity, a database update, feedback on the home, other sellers they know, and finance. One call, five openings.\n- Agree the invitation with the listing agent. On the Campaign Snapshot, under When it lists, tick \"Anything else\" and note the date."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "just-listed-email",
  "title": "Just Listed email (private viewing)",
  "kind": "email",
  "when": "Email every database contact who lives near a new listing on the day you door knock the street, a few days after the letter, offering a private look before it goes live.",
  "body": "Subject: Just listed near you: [address]\n\nDear [first name],\n\nRay White Mildura has just listed [address], close to your home.\n\nBefore it goes live to the public, I would like to offer you a private look. If you would like to see it first, reply to this email or give me a call.\n\nRegards,\n[your name]\nRay White Mildura, [mobile]\n\n[Property description: two or three sentences from the listing copy, with the main photo, pasted under the sign-off.]\n\n## Before you send\n\n- Keep it this short. Three lines, a sign-off, then the property. The offer is the private look, not the description.\n- Send to database contacts in the farm or within a few streets of the listing, not the whole database. \"Close to your home\" has to be true.\n- If there is a neighbours' preview, add the day and time: \"This [day] at [time].\"\n- Every reply is a buyer appointment or an appraisal conversation. Call them back the same day; do not just email.\n- On the Campaign Snapshot, under When it lists, tick \"Email to nearby database contacts\" and note the date and how many went out."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "just-sold-email",
  "title": "Just Sold email",
  "kind": "email",
  "when": "Email every database contact near a property once the Just Sold letter has landed and you have knocked the street, with the free appraisal offer; follow up with a phone call to the buyers who inspected.",
  "body": "Subject: Sold near you: [address]\n\nHi [first name],\n\nGood news: we have just sold [address], not far from you.\n\nBuyer interest is strong at the moment, and once again several buyers missed out on this home.\n\nWhen we take a home to market, we talk with buyers who want to live in this area. Because we have been part of the local market for a long time, buyers trust us to know how it is tracking, and we work hard to give them better service than they expect.\n\nCurious where your home sits in today's market? For a free, no-obligation appraisal, call me directly on [mobile] or drop into the office at [office address].\n\nKind regards,\n[your name]\nRay White Mildura\n\n## Before you send\n\n- Only say \"several buyers missed out\" if the listing agent confirms it. On most sales there are some, and the real number powers the Just Sold phone call: \"2 people who looked at the home missed out\".\n- Add the sale price only if the vendor has agreed it can be disclosed.\n- Same list as the Just Listed email: contacts near the property, not everyone.\n- The email is the fourth touch of the Just Sold campaign (letter, door knock, phone, email, SMS). On the Campaign Snapshot, under When it sells, tick \"Email to nearby database contacts\".\n- Every reply gets a call within the hour, with two times offered for the appraisal."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "monday-market-note",
  "title": "Monday Market Note (weekend results)",
  "kind": "email",
  "when": "Send every Monday before 10am to every seller on your pipeline board except Lost, built from Ray White Mildura's weekend results; whatever their tier, every card on the board gets it.",
  "body": "Subject: Monday Market Note: [Mildura / suburb] this week\n\nMorning [first name],\n\n[Market line for the week, from the office's figures. For example: \"[n] homes sold across Mildura and surrounds last week\" or \"[n] new listings came on the market this week\". If the office reports auctions, you can use the clearance rate: \"[x]% of the [n] auctions held in [region] sold.\"]\n\n[Optional comparison: \"That compares with [the same figure] at this time last year.\" Leave it out if the office does not have the number.]\n\nHere at Ray White Mildura we sold [n] of our [n] [auctions / homes on the market] over the weekend. Buyers were active on every one of them, and [n] buyers who missed out are still looking in your area.\n\n- [address], [suburb] $[price or undisclosed]\n- [address], [suburb] $[price or undisclosed]\n- [address], [suburb] $[price or undisclosed]\n- [address], [suburb] $[price or undisclosed]\n- [address], [suburb] $[price or undisclosed]\n\n[Optional close: \"Want to know what these results mean for your place? Reply to this email or call me on [mobile].\"]\n\n[your name]\nRay White Mildura, [mobile]\n\n## Example in our voice\n\n> Morning Sharon and Dave. [n] homes sold across Mildura and surrounds last week, against [n] at the same time last year. Our office sold [n] of [n] over the weekend, with buyers active on all of them, and [n] buyers who missed out are still looking near you.\n\n> [address], Irymple $[price]. [address], Mildura South $[price]. [address], Red Cliffs undisclosed. [address], Merbein $[price].\n\n## Rules (office guidance)\n\n- Every number comes from the office's Monday results, checked before you send. Nothing from memory, nothing rounded up.\n- Only list a sale price the vendor has agreed can be disclosed. Otherwise write \"undisclosed\".\n- \"Buyers who missed out are still looking in your area\" is the line that creates appraisals. Write it only when it is true, then ring your 1 to 2 month pipeline contacts and ask the Timing Question.\n- Keep it short enough to read on one phone screen. If it runs long, cut the market line, not the sales list.\n- Send it from your own email address so replies come to you, and log every reply as a touch in the CRM."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "one-line-checkin-sms",
  "title": "The One-Line Check-in",
  "kind": "sms",
  "when": "Send to a database or pipeline contact two to six times a year (by tier) as the simplest way to check whether they are still thinking of selling.",
  "body": "Hi [first name], is selling still on the cards for you this year?\n\n[your name]\n\n## How to use it\n\n- One question. No pitch, no link, no signature block. That is why people answer it.\n- Works as an SMS or an email. Contact Tiers set how many a year: 2 for Bronze and Silver, 4 for Gold, 6 for Platinum.\n- \"Yes\" or \"maybe\": call within the hour and book the Doorstep Appraisal. \"Does 2pm or 4pm suit?\"\n- \"No\" or \"not this year\": thank them, update the timeframe on their record, and keep the next scheduled touch.\n- No reply: do not chase. The next touch in their schedule will come around.\n- Send it from your own mobile in your admin block, never during a call session, and log it as a touch in the CRM."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "appraisal-sms-30-prior",
  "title": "Appraisal SMS: 30 minutes before",
  "kind": "sms",
  "when": "Send 30 minutes before every booked appraisal; schedule it the day you book.",
  "body": "> Hi [first name], just letting you know I'm about 30 minutes away from our appointment today. Looking forward to meeting you then. [your name], Ray White Mildura\n\n## How to use it\n\n- Schedule it in the CRM or on your phone the day you book the appraisal, so it still goes when you are driving.\n- It does three jobs. It confirms the appointment, it cuts no-shows, and it starts the clock. You promised about 15 minutes; arriving on time is the first proof.\n- If they reply to cancel, offer two new times straight away: \"Would tomorrow at 2pm or 4pm suit?\"\n- Keep it this short. It is a heads-up, not a sales message."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "appraisal-sms-2-hours-after",
  "title": "Appraisal SMS: 2 hours after",
  "kind": "sms",
  "when": "Send two hours after every appraisal, before any letter or call; it closes the visit and opens the 30-day trust plan.",
  "body": "> Hi [first name], it was great to meet you today. Whenever you're ready, I'd love to help with your property plans. Regards, [your name], Ray White Mildura\n\n## How to use it\n\n- Two hours after, not two days. Set it while you are still in the car filling in the Quick Appraisal form.\n- Write the words out in full. No text shorthand.\n- Day 0 ends with this SMS. Day 1 is a call: for a Hot lead whose appraisal is done, thank them and answer any questions from the visit; for a Warm lead, ask what has them thinking about selling. Day 2 is the Introduction letter for Cold and Warm leads.\n- If they reply with a question, that is the start of a conversation. Answer it by phone, not by text."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "appraisal-sms-anniversary",
  "title": "Appraisal SMS: 12-month anniversary",
  "kind": "sms",
  "when": "Send 12 months after every appraisal, in the same week as the 12-month re-appraisal letter; set both dates the day you log the appraisal.",
  "body": "> Hi [first name], it's been a year since your appraisal. If you'd like a free update on what your home is worth today, just reply YES and I'll drop by. Regards, [your name], Ray White Mildura\n\n## How to use it\n\n- We say \"appraisal\", never \"valuation\". Only a licensed valuer gives a valuation. This is a compliance rule, not a style choice.\n- Set the date the day you log the appraisal: 12 months out, the same week as the 12-month re-appraisal letter.\n- Every YES is a Doorstep Appraisal and restarts the 12-month clock. One text message books next year's appraisal for you.\n- No reply: the letter has gone too. Leave it. The next touch in their tier will come around."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "just-listed-sms",
  "title": "Just Listed SMS (neighbours' preview)",
  "kind": "sms",
  "when": "Text database contacts near a new listing on the day you door knock the street, a few days after the letter, inviting them to the neighbours' preview before launch.",
  "body": "> Hi [first name], hope all is well. A courtesy heads-up: [address] near you is now for sale, so you may see a few more cars in the street. We'd love you to come to a neighbours' preview before it goes public: this [day] at [time]. Hope to see you there. [your name], Ray White Mildura\n\n## How to use it\n\n- Keep every SMS under 300 characters. With a typical address this one lands at about 290, so if a long address pushes it over, trim the courtesy line, never the day and time.\n- Send only to contacts near the listing who have a mobile in the CRM. The rest get the letter.\n- Three parts matter: the courtesy reason (extra traffic), the invitation, and the day and time. Without the day and time there is nothing to say yes to.\n- On the Campaign Snapshot, under When it lists, tick \"Texts sent\" and note the date and how many went out."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "just-sold-sms",
  "title": "Just Sold SMS (buyers still looking)",
  "kind": "sms",
  "when": "Text database contacts near a property once the Just Sold letter has landed and you have knocked the street; the buyers-still-looking line turns it into a referral and appraisal question, not an announcement.",
  "body": "> Hi [first name], hope your day's going well. We've just sold [address] near you and still have 2 buyers looking in the area. Do you know anyone thinking of selling? [your name], Ray White Mildura\n\n## How to use it\n\n- Change \"2 buyers\" to the real number the listing agent gives you.\n- About 200 characters, comfortably inside our 300-character SMS guide.\n- It ends with a question, so expect replies. Call back every reply the same day. \"Do you know anyone?\" often turns into \"Well, we might be.\"\n- Send to contacts near the sale who have a mobile; the letter covers the rest of the street.\n- On the Campaign Snapshot, under When it sells, tick \"Texts sent\". The Just Sold campaign runs letter, door knock, phone, email, SMS."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "daily-call-sheet",
  "title": "Daily Call Sheet",
  "kind": "form",
  "when": "Print one every morning and cross off every dial, connection and appointment as it happens; the totals go into the app at the end of the day.",
  "body": "## Dials (cross off a box each time you dial)\n\n- Dials 1 to 10: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Dials 11 to 20: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Dials 21 to 30: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Dials 31 to 40: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Dials 41 to 50: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Dials 51 to 60: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Dials 61 to 70: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Dials 71 to 80: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n\n## Connections (a real conversation with the owner, not a voicemail)\n\n- Connections 1 to 10: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Connections 11 to 20: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Connections 21 to 30: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Connections 31 to 40: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Connections 41 to 50: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Connections 51 to 60: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Connections 61 to 70: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Connections 71 to 80: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n\n## Appointments (a booked time, in the diary)\n\n- Appointments 1 to 10: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Appointments 11 to 20: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n\n## Notes\n\n- Session 1: started [time], finished [time]\n- Session 2: started [time], finished [time]\n- New geo data today: [number]\n- Appointments booked (name, address, day, time): [list]\n- Callbacks owed (name, number, when): [list]\n- One thing I will do differently tomorrow: [note]\n\n## Grade each 45-minute session (office guidance)\n\nThe program's benchmarks for every 45-minute session:\n\n- 18 to 24 calls\n- 8 to 12 connections\n- 1 to 3 appointments\n\nGreen: all three in range. Amber: calls in range, but connections or appointments short. Red: fewer than 18 calls. A red session is nearly always a preparation problem, not a skill problem. Was your list of 30 names ready before you sat down?\n\n## How to use it\n\n- Paper on the desk, pen in hand, phone only. No computer during the session.\n- There is room for 80 dials, 80 connections and 20 appointments: two strong sessions with room to spare.\n- At the end of the day, enter the three totals in the app's call-session counters and tap + for each new geo data record. Call notes go into the CRM in your admin block, not between calls.\n- Keep your sheets. Four weeks of them show your pattern better than memory can, and Damian or Luke will ask to see them."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "call-session-rules",
  "title": "Call Session checklist",
  "kind": "checklist",
  "when": "Run through it before and after every call session; it is the one-page method every session is graded against.",
  "body": "> Protect your call sessions first. Everything else in the day fits around them.\n\n## Before the session\n\n- Preparation decides the session. Most poor sessions are lost before the first dial.\n- Have 30 names and numbers ready before every session.\n- Build the list the day before, from your farm, in the admin block. Never during the session.\n- Daily Call Sheet printed. Pen, phone, notepad or voice recorder. Computer closed.\n- Know why you are calling before you dial: the free market report, a Fifty Neighbours event, the quarterly market update, or a One-Line Check-in follow-up.\n\n## During the session\n\n- 45 minutes on, 15 minutes off.\n- Capture notes on a notepad or a voice recorder as you go.\n- The Two-Minute Close: once a call passes two minutes, start steering toward an appointment and book it.\n- Always offer two times. \"Does 2pm or 4pm suit?\"\n- No computer. Calls flow better without a screen in front of you, which is why the preparation happens the day before.\n- Rejection is not personal. Jot the likely reason (bad timing, no need, the approach did not land) and move to the next name.\n\n## Benchmarks per 45-minute session\n\n- 18 to 24 calls\n- 8 to 12 connections\n- 1 to 3 appointments\n\n## After the session\n\n- Admin (call notes) happens in its own block, away from call sessions and appointments.\n- Top agents hand their admin to someone else and spend their hours on work that earns. For now you do your own, but always in its own block and never inside a session.\n- Enter every new contact into the CRM as geo data: name, phone and/or email, address, source and date.\n- Put every appointment in the diary and the CRM before you leave the desk.\n- Log calls, connections, appointments and geo data in the app.\n\n## Launchpad rhythm (office guidance)\n\n- Weeks 1 and 2: one session a day.\n- From week 3: two sessions a day. Session 1 at 9:00, a break, session 2 at 10:00, then the admin block.\n- Two green sessions a day, five days a week, is what drives 300 / 30 / 3. Nothing else in the program works without it."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "quick-appraisal-form",
  "title": "Quick Appraisal form",
  "kind": "form",
  "when": "Fill in within 2 minutes of every kerbside or Doorstep Appraisal, in the car before you drive off; an appraisal is not complete until it has an action plan, a category and a precinct.",
  "body": "## Owner\n\n- Agent: [your name]\n- Date: [date]\n- Owner's name: [owner name]\n- Best phone: [phone]\n- Email: [email]\n- Where the owner lives: [address]\n- Already in the CRM? Yes / No\n\n## The property\n\n- Address: [address] (same as above? Yes / No)\n- Suburb and postcode: [suburb] [postcode]\n- Bedrooms [n], bathrooms [n], car spaces [n]\n- Type: house, unit, rural, semi-rural or acreage, vacant land, commercial, business\n- Land [m2], house [squares]\n\n## Notes\n\n- [What you saw, what they said, and anything personal to remember for the next call]\n\n## Action plan\n\n- Precinct (always fill in): [precinct or street group]\n- Pick one: follow-up trial at 3, 6, 9 or 12 months / unsuccessful / listed / no letter needed, onto the 12-month re-appraisal plan\n\n## Launchpad fields\n\n- Kerbside or walk-through: [which]\n- Price range given: $[low] to $[high]\n- Lead group: Cold / Warm / Hot\n- Pipeline category: 1 to 2 months / 2 to 12 months / not selling\n- Next touch (date and mode): [date, call / visit / SMS / letter / video]\n- 12-month re-appraisal letter date: [date]\n- Anniversary SMS date: [date]\n- 2-hours-after SMS: sent / scheduled\n\n## Rules\n\n- No appraisal is complete without an action plan, a category and a precinct. It does not count toward your 30 until it has all three.\n- When nothing is happening, the default is \"No letter needed, onto the 12-month re-appraisal plan\". That is how next year's appraisals book themselves.\n- Not selling? In the app, tap + for the appraisal, then tap \"Skip, just count it\". The detail sheet is only for 1 to 2 or 2 to 12 month timeframes, because saving it puts the owner on your pipeline.\n- Full walk-through? Use the Full Appraisal form instead. This one is for kerbside and quick Doorstep Appraisals.\n- \"Already in the CRM? No\" means this visit is also a piece of geo data. Add the record tonight."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "full-appraisal-form",
  "title": "Full Appraisal form (walk-through)",
  "kind": "form",
  "when": "Use for every walk-through appraisal; see the home the way an agent does in about 15 minutes and leave with a range, four comparable sales, an action plan and a category.",
  "body": "## Owner\n\n- Agent: [your name]\n- Date: [date]\n- Owner's name: [owner name]\n- Best phone: [phone]\n- Email: [email]\n- Where the owner lives: [address]\n- Already in the CRM? Yes / No\n\n## The property\n\n- Address: [address] (same as above? Yes / No)\n- Suburb and postcode: [suburb] [postcode]\n- Price range: $[low] to $[high]\n- Type: house, unit, rural, semi-rural or acreage, vacant land, commercial, business\n- Land [m2], house [squares]\n\n## Rooms\n\n- Bedrooms: [number]. Robes in each: walk-in / built-in / none\n- Bathrooms: [number]. Toilets: [number]\n- Ensuite: none / semi / full / separate\n- Family or rumpus room: Yes / No. Study: Yes / No\n- Notes on the kitchen: [ ]\n- Notes on the lounge: [ ]\n- Notes on the dining area: [ ]\n- Notes on the laundry: [ ]\n\n## Appliances and fittings\n\n- Hot water: electric / gas / solar\n- Oven: electric / gas / wall oven. Cooktop: electric / gas\n- Dishwasher: Yes / No\n- Cooling: Yes / No. Heating: Yes / No\n- Floor coverings: carpet / tiles / polished boards / lino\n\n## Outside\n\n- Garage spaces: [n]. Carport spaces: [n]. Off-street parks: [n]. Sheds: [n]\n- Side or rear access: Yes / No\n- Yard and outdoor areas: [ ]\n\n## How it is built\n\n- Walls: brick veneer / weatherboard / fibro / stone / render or Conite\n- Floor: concrete slab / timber\n- Roof: tin / tile\n- Builder: [ ]. Year built: [ ]\n\n## Four comparable sales (last six months)\n\n- 1. [address, sale date, price]\n- 2. [address, sale date, price]\n- 3. [address, sale date, price]\n- 4. [address, sale date, price]\n\n## Appraisal type\n\n- Walk-through / Kerbside\n\n## Action plan (pick one)\n\n- Follow-up plan: 1, 3, 6 or 12 months, or the anniversary plan\n- Or: on the market / unsuccessful / no letter needed\n\n## Category\n\n- Pipeline: 1 to 2 months / 2 to 12 months\n- Precinct: [ ]\n- Lead group: Cold / Warm / Hot\n\n## Notes\n\n- [ ]\n\n## How to use it (office guidance)\n\n- About 15 minutes in the home, four comparable sales from the last six months, and always a range, kept conservative. Say it the way the Doorstep Script does: the range is a careful estimate, and with the right campaign in this market you could do better than that.\n- Fill the form in the car, not in the home. In the home you are building the relationship; the form is for your memory.\n- Pricing method is our office guidance: pick the three or four closest sales (same suburb, similar land, bedrooms and build, sold in the last six months), adjust for condition, and quote the range they bracket. Unsure? Say so, and check with Damian or Luke before you quote a figure.\n- Every full form leaves with an action plan and a category. That decides the pipeline column and the 30-day trust plan the owner goes into.\n- Practise on a colleague's home or an office open home in week 4 until you can complete it in under 10 minutes."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "trust-plan-self-check",
  "title": "The Self-Check (10 ticks)",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Score yourself at the week 6 halfway review, again at the end of week 7 and at graduation; fewer than seven ticks means your leads are running on memory, not on a plan.",
  "body": "Tick a box only if it is true right now.\n\n- 01 I can name every listing lead I have for the next 30 days.\n- 02 For each of those leads, I know whether they trust me yet.\n- 03 What I send cold leads is different from what I send warm and hot leads.\n- 04 No listing has gone to another agent purely over fees in the past three months.\n- 05 Every new lead has a written 30-day plan of what they will get from me.\n- 06 At least once a week I send a personal video, not just a property video.\n- 07 I have a recent result ready to show a cold lead who needs proof.\n- 08 AI writes the first draft of my emails and video scripts.\n- 09 New leads start their plan the same day we meet.\n- 10 I have help in the office to keep my plans running.\n\nMy score: [ticks] out of 10\n\n> Fewer than seven ticks? Then your leads are running on memory. The 30-day trust plans fix that.\n\n## When to score it\n\n- Week 6 (your baseline, for the halfway Checkpoint Conversation), week 7 (on Friday, once every lead is tagged) and week 12 (graduation). Write each score on this sheet and show Damian or Luke.\n- Target: 7 or more by week 6, 9 or more by week 12.\n- Every blank becomes a task card with a due date. The gaps are your plan for the next six weeks.\n\n## Notes for a new associate (office guidance)\n\n- Item 04: if no listing has gone elsewhere over the fee yet, tick it. By week 12 you will have asked for listings, so the tick has to be earned.\n- Item 07: you may not have a result of your own yet. An office result you can describe in detail (the street, what the owners wanted, how it finished) counts, with the listing agent's OK.\n- Item 10: that is Damian or Luke, plus the buddy you name on your 30-Day Commitment."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "thirty-day-commitment",
  "title": "The 30-Day Commitment",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Sign it in week 1 with honest zeros, fill in lines 1 and 2 in week 7, complete every line at Checkpoint 2 in week 8, and sign a fresh one at graduation; send Damian or Luke a photo each time you sign.",
  "body": "At Checkpoint 2, complete every line before you leave the room.\n\n- 1. Listing leads I have for the next 30 days: [number]\n- 2. Of those, Cold / Warm / Hot: [n] / [n] / [n]\n- 3. Date my first video goes out: [day and date]\n- 4. Three leads I am adding to a trust plan this week: [name], [name], [name]\n- 5. First email I will draft with AI: [which group, which emotion]\n- 6. My weekly filming slot: [day, time]\n- 7. The person in the office who helps me run the plans: [name]\n- 8. The person holding me to this: [Damian / Luke / buddy]\n- 9. Date we review it: [date]\n\nSigned: [your name]. Date: [date]\n\n## How to use it\n\n- Week 1: write 0 on lines 1 and 2 if that is today's truth, and leave the video and email lines until The 30-Day Trust Plan module. Set review dates at weeks 4, 8 and 12.\n- Line 1 is your live pipeline, by name. If you cannot write the number without opening the CRM, fix that first.\n- Line 2 should add up to line 1. Mostly Cold leads means this month is about giving value. Mostly Hot leads means it is about asking for the next step.\n- Line 6 goes in the diary, not just on this page. Same day, same time, every week, for the rest of the program.\n- Lines 7 to 9 are the accountability. Your next 30 days start on Monday.\n- At graduation, sign a fresh one with a review date 30 days out. That is your month 4."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "campaign-snapshot",
  "title": "The Campaign Snapshot",
  "kind": "form",
  "when": "Open one the day any listing in or near your farm goes live (yours, or an office listing you are working); tick each channel when it lists and when it sells, and track results for 12 weeks.",
  "body": "- Property: [address]\n- Agent: [your name]\n- Launch date: [date]\n- Sale date: [date]\n\n## When it lists (tick each channel, with the date)\n\n- Letters in letterboxes: [date, how many homes]\n- Doors knocked: [date, how many]\n- Calls made: [date, how many]\n- Email to nearby database contacts: [date, how many sent]\n- Texts sent: [date, how many]\n- Anything else (neighbours' preview, social post, open home invitation): [date]\n\n## When it sells (tick each channel, with the date)\n\n- Letters in letterboxes: [date, how many homes]\n- Doors knocked: [date, how many]\n- Calls made: [date, how many]\n- Email to nearby database contacts: [date, how many sent]\n- Texts sent: [date, how many]\n- Anything else (callbacks to buyers who inspected): [date]\n\n## Results, week by week (weeks 1 to 12)\n\n- New listings won: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Appraisals booked: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- New pipeline leads: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- New contacts added (geo data): [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Landlords met: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Inspections: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n- Other: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]\n\n## Debrief with Damian or Luke\n\n> \"Let's go through the campaign at [address]. Here's your Snapshot. How does it compare with your cold-calling numbers? What worked, what didn't, and what will you change on the next one?\"\n\n- What worked? [ ]\n- What did not? [ ]\n- What will you change next time? [ ]\n\n## How to use it\n\n- Twelve touches per listing: six channels when it lists, six when it sells. The aim is for every listing to lead to 1 to 2 more.\n- Every YES at the door or on the phone is an appraisal booking. Log it here and in the CRM the same day, then tap + for the appraisal in the app once you have done it.\n- In week 10, compare this campaign's warm conversion (appraisals per 100 doors) with your cold geo-data ratios. That comparison is the lesson."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "monday-meeting-agenda",
  "title": "Monday Meeting agenda",
  "kind": "checklist",
  "when": "Run it in this order every Monday with Damian or Luke; from week 11 you run it yourself.",
  "body": "The Monday Meeting, in order.\n\n## Stock\n\n- What has happened on each of our listings this week?\n\n## Open homes\n\n- Which homes are open this week, and when?\n\n## Pipeline\n\n- Which pipeline contacts are you following up this week, and when do you expect them to list?\n\n## Missed listings\n\n- Did anyone in our database list with another agency this week?\n\n## Settlements\n\n- Which sales settle this week?\n\n## This week's focus\n\n- Looking at your Scoreboard, which activities are you focusing on this week?\n\n## Priority buyers\n\n- Who are our most active buyers right now, and what do they need to hear from us this week?\n\n## Marketing\n\n- What marketing do you, or we, need to get done this week?\n\n## Recap\n\n- Read back the key actions for the week.\n- What support do you need?\n- Is it all achievable?\n- Are you committing to it?\n\n## Launchpad additions (office guidance)\n\n- 300 / 30 / 3 pace: geo data, appraisals and listings this week and to date, against the soft guide and the pace needed from here on the app's Progress tab.\n- Session ratios for the week: calls, connections and appointments against 18 to 24 / 8 to 12 / 1 to 3. Name the one ratio to lift.\n- Overdue touches: any pipeline contact past its weekly (1 to 2 months) or monthly (2 to 12 months) touch.\n- Street Appraisal Days: which street is this week, and have the letters landed?\n- Just Listed / Just Sold: any office listing or sale in or near the farm this week? That means a block of Fifty Neighbours calls and a Campaign Snapshot.\n- Bring your Monday Plan filled in. The meeting is for decisions, not for filling in sheets.\n- Twenty minutes. Stand up if it helps."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "monday-plan",
  "title": "The Monday Plan",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Fill in before every Monday meeting, three lines per section; the office puts $5 on each weekly focus line as a stake.",
  "body": "Three lines per section, filled in before the meeting. A sample entry sits under each heading to show the standard. Swap the brackets for your real addresses and names.\n\n## Stock: changes and updates\n\n- 1. [property: what it needs this week]\n- 2. [property: what it needs this week]\n- 3. [property: what it needs this week]\n\n> Sample: [address], Irymple. Two private inspections needed this week. [address], Mildura. Price adjusted last week, 10 through and 2 offers, so go back to both buyers for their best. [address], Red Cliffs. 8 through last week; now needs feedback to the vendor and a firm offer.\n\n## Open homes this week\n\n- 1. [address, day, time]\n- 2. [address, day, time]\n- 3. [address, day, time]\n\n> Sample: [address], Tuesday 5:00 to 5:30. [address], Wednesday 5:00 to 5:30. [address], Saturday 10:00 to 10:30.\n\n## Pipeline follow-ups, next 7 days\n\n- 1. [name or address: the touch and why]\n- 2. [name or address: the touch and why]\n- 3. [name or address: the touch and why]\n\n> Sample: [name]: private inspection Thursday. [name]: call about our sale two doors down. [name]: listing presentation Wednesday at 5pm.\n\n## Other agencies' listings\n\n- 1. [address: what you will do about it]\n- 2. [address: what you will do about it]\n- 3. [address: what you will do about it]\n\n> Sample: [address]: knock on the neighbours' doors about our nearby sale. [address]: check in with the owner and mention our [street] result.\n\n## Priority buyers\n\n- 1. [name: what they need to hear this week]\n- 2. [name: what they need to hear this week]\n- 3. [name: what they need to hear this week]\n\n> Sample: [buyer]: call about the new Irymple listing. [buyers]: still want something central, so send the two new ones. [buyer]: book a second look at [address].\n\n## Settlements\n\n- 1. [address, date]\n- 2. [address, date]\n- 3. [address, date]\n\n> Sample: [address], [date]. [address], [date]. [address], [date].\n\n## This week's focus ($5 on each line, see office guidance below)\n\n- [focus 1] $5\n- [focus 2] $5\n- [focus 3] $5\n\n> Sample: Call 90% of my database list $5. 4 appraisals booked $5. 2 inspections from each open home $5.\n\n## Social media and letterbox drops\n\n- [post or drop, who, by when]\n\n> Sample: Facebook post on the weekend's results. Just Sold cards to 100 homes in [street], Merbein, then door knock Thursday.\n\n## For a new associate (office guidance)\n\n- Stock: you may have none yet. Write in the office listing you are working for Just Listed or Just Sold.\n- Your three focus lines should be the ones that move 300 / 30 / 3. For example: \"Two green sessions a day, five days $5\", \"Every door on this week's Street Appraisal street knocked $5\", \"Every Hot lead gets the ask-for-the-listing call $5\".\n- How the $5 works in our office: miss the line and the $5 goes in the office jar. Say each line out loud in the meeting. Saying it in front of the team is the point of the stake."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "targets-vs-actuals",
  "title": "Targets vs Actuals (8 measures)",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Update every Monday from week 1 and build your own month-4 version in week 11; it is the Scoreboard page Damian and Luke read first.",
  "body": "## The eight measures\n\n- Lead measures (the soft numbers: the activity you control day to day): database growth, prospecting calls, pipeline additions, appraisals\n- Result measures (the hard numbers: what follows from them): listing appointments, listings, sales, commission\n\n## Benchmarks for an established agent\n\n- Database growth: 14 a week, 56 a month\n- Prospecting calls: 20 a week, 80 a month\n- Pipeline additions: 4 a week, 16 a month\n- Appraisals: 4 a week, 16 a month\n- Listing appointments: 4 a week, 20 a month\n- Listings: 1 a week, 4 a month\n- Sales: 1 a week, 4 a month\n- Commission: $3,000 a week, $12,000 a month\n\nFor each measure the sheet tracks the weekly and monthly targets, weeks 1 to 4, the total, and what is still to go.\n\n## Your Launchpad sheet (one row per measure, filled in each Monday)\n\n- Database growth (geo data): weekly target [n], monthly target [n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], still to go [ ]\n- Prospecting calls: weekly target [n], monthly target [n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], still to go [ ]\n- Pipeline additions (appraisals that went onto your pipeline with a timeframe: 1 to 2 months or 2 to 12 months): weekly target [n], monthly target [n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], still to go [ ]\n- Appraisals (a Doorstep Appraisal or a full walk-through): weekly target [n], monthly target [n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], still to go [ ]\n- Listing appointments (the meeting where you ask for the business): weekly target [n], monthly target [n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], still to go [ ]\n- Listings: weekly target [n], monthly target [n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], still to go [ ]\n- Sales: weekly target [n], monthly target [n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], still to go [ ]\n- Commission: weekly target $[n], monthly target $[n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], still to go [ ]\n\n## Setting your numbers (office guidance)\n\n- 300 geo data over 12 weeks is about 25 a week, or about 100 a month. That is well above the benchmark of 14 a week because you are building from zero. That is the program.\n- 30 appraisals over 12 weeks is 2 to 3 a week. The benchmark for an established agent is 4 a week. Aim to get there by month 3.\n- Calls: the 20 a week above is an established agent's figure, not yours. Your sessions set your number: at 18 to 24 calls per 45-minute session, two sessions a day for five days is roughly 180 to 240 calls a week. Set the target from the sessions you are really running.\n- Listings: 3 across the program, the first around week 8, then about one a fortnight (the app's guide expects them around weeks 8, 10 and 12). Listing appointments: every Hot lead on day 15 of their trust plan.\n- Commission: ask Damian or Luke for the office's average commission per sale, then multiply by your expected sales. Do not guess.\n- The app works out your exact pace. This sheet shows the pattern: which lead measure is short when a result measure is short.\n\n## Year to date\n\n- January to December totals for each measure, plus the monthly average.\n- Commission targets against actuals, by month, quarter and year.\n- Start this in week 11 so you leave the program with month 4 already planned."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "preferred-partners",
  "title": "Preferred Partners list",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Fill in over weeks 7 and 8, three calls a week, until all ten partners have a name, a number and an agreed client discount.",
  "body": "> Every seller has a to-do list before the listing goes live. Give them the right people to tick it off. Few agents ever do.\n\n> Ask every partner below for a 10% discount for our clients. It is one more reason to choose us.\n\n## The ten partners\n\n- Painter: patch, touch up and freshen the walls before the photographer comes. Name and number: [ ]. 10% agreed: Yes / No\n- Handyman: the small fixes buyers spot at the open. Name and number: [ ]. 10% agreed: Yes / No\n- Landscaper or gardener: street appeal, because the front yard is the first photo. Name and number: [ ]. 10% agreed: Yes / No\n- Builder: bigger repairs, and advice on what is worth doing before a sale. Name and number: [ ]. 10% agreed: Yes / No\n- Cleaner: a top-to-bottom clean ahead of the photographer and the first open. Name and number: [ ]. 10% agreed: Yes / No\n- Stylist: furniture hire and styling so the home photographs well and shows well. Name and number: [ ]. 10% agreed: Yes / No\n- Building and pest inspector: no nasty surprises when a buyer gets serious. Name and number: [ ]. 10% agreed: Yes / No\n- Conveyancer or solicitor: the contract ready before launch day. Name and number: [ ]. 10% agreed: Yes / No\n- Mortgage broker: buyers who know their borrowing limit before they make an offer. Name and number: [ ]. 10% agreed: Yes / No\n- Removalist and storage: room to show the home, and help with the move. Name and number: [ ]. 10% agreed: Yes / No\n\n## The three I will call this week about the 10%\n\n- 1. [partner, name]\n- 2. [partner, name]\n- 3. [partner, name]\n\n## Office guidance\n\n- Check with Damian or Luke first. The office may already have a broker, conveyancer and storage partner. They are the ones named in the Broker, Legal and Storage letters. Use those.\n- Where the list gets used: the Presentation letter (day 14), the Legal letter (day 18), the Broker letter (day 21), the Storage letter (day 24), and every \"what should I fix before I sell?\" conversation at the door.\n- Ask each partner to send work your way too. A painter who is in three Mildura homes a week meets sellers before you do."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "video-library",
  "title": "The video library (six evergreen videos)",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Write four bullet points for each video in week 8, film all six in one sitting, and add the links to the trust plan days that call for a video.",
  "body": "> Six short videos you film once and reuse in every trust plan as a link. Write four bullet points for each, then film them all in one sitting.\n\n## The six\n\n- Education. MARKET UPDATE. \"[number] homes sold this week and [number] people came through the opens. Here is what that tells you if selling is on your mind.\" My four points: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]. Filmed on: [date]\n- Fears. WHAT THE FEE PAYS FOR. \"Everything that happens between listing day and settlement day, and who does it.\" My four points: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]. Filmed on: [date]\n- Proof. A RECENT RESULT. \"The story of [address]: where the owners started, our plan, and the result.\" My four points: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]. Filmed on: [date]\n- Fears. CHOOSING AN AGENT. \"The three questions I would ask any agent, me included, before signing anything.\" My four points: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]. Filmed on: [date]\n- Personality. WHY I DO THIS. \"Half a minute on what drew me to this work, and what clients can always count on from me.\" My four points: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]. Filmed on: [date]\n- Hot leads. THE ASK. \"We have just sold [address] for [result] and buyers are still looking near you. Can we catch up this week?\" My four points: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]. Filmed on: [date]\n\n## From script to sent, in six steps\n\n- 1. Get Claude or ChatGPT to write a first draft of the script.\n- 2. Have it boiled down to four bullet points.\n- 3. Open a video app with on-screen notes (VideoAsk works on iPhone, BigVU on Android).\n- 4. Paste your four points into the notes.\n- 5. Press record and speak to your points in a single take.\n- 6. Send the link by SMS, by email or through the CRM.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Keep it under 60 seconds.\n- Speak to one person, the way you would across a kitchen table.\n- Good light, a quiet room, the phone at eye level.\n- Sent is better than perfect. Get the first one out this week.\n\n## Office guidance for a new associate\n\n- The Market Update is your first one-to-one video (week 8) and your weekly one from then on. It is not the six-beat monthly video you first filmed in week 4. The numbers come from the office's weekly results; check every one before you film.\n- A Recent Result: use an office sale with the listing agent's OK, and say it is the office's result, not yours.\n- The Market Update dates within a week. The other five last; film them once, properly, and reuse the links for months.\n- Where the links go: the trust plan days that call for a video (Cold plan days 4 and 17, Warm plan days 4 and 17, Hot plan day 7)."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "letter-schedule",
  "title": "The letter schedule",
  "kind": "checklist",
  "when": "Personalise all six letters in week 6, have each one signed off as Ready, then send them by group and day after every appraisal.",
  "body": "> Six letters, written once. Each speaks to one emotion and goes on a set day. Hardly anyone posts a letter now, which is exactly why people read them.\n\n## The schedule\n\n- Day 2, Introduction. Speaks to: personality. Goes to: Cold and Warm leads. Job: says thank you, and shows you work differently. Ready: [ ]\n- Day 9, Testimonial. Speaks to: proof. Goes to: Cold leads. Job: what past clients say, with phone numbers they are happy for you to ring. Ready: [ ]\n- Day 14, Presentation. Speaks to: education. Goes to: Warm and Hot leads. Job: how to present the home, plus your trusted local trades. Ready: [ ]\n- Day 18, Legal. Speaks to: fears. Goes to: Hot leads. Job: explains who does the legal work and names local conveyancers. Ready: [ ]\n- Day 21, Broker. Speaks to: fears. Goes to: Hot leads. Job: how our broker partner helps buyers stretch further. Ready: [ ]\n- Day 24, Storage. Speaks to: education. Goes to: Hot leads. Job: more room to show the home, with a special rate for our clients. Ready: [ ]\n\n> Put your name, mobile and partner details in the brackets, then load the letters into the CRM alongside the three 30-day trust plans.\n\n## Launchpad schedule (office guidance)\n\n- Day 0 is the appraisal or visit. Day 2 means two days after you met them.\n- Week 6: personalise all six with Ray White Mildura details, your name, title and number, and the broker and storage partners. Damian or Luke sign each one off as Ready before it goes.\n- Enclosures to build once: the presentation tips sheet, three conveyancers with contact names, three to five office testimonials with permission and numbers, and the broker and storage offers.\n- Letters and emails work alongside your calls and visits, and the letter sets up the call. About a week after each letter lands, your touch-point call asks whether it arrived and what they thought.\n- Hand-write the envelopes. Post them rather than hand-delivering, unless you are already knocking that street."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "trust-plan-sequences",
  "title": "The three 30-day trust plans (day by day)",
  "kind": "checklist",
  "when": "Load all three into the CRM in week 8, then start every new lead on the right plan the day you meet them; tick each touch as it is done.",
  "body": "> Three 30-day trust plans, one for each lead group, using six ways to reach people. Load them into the CRM once and every new lead starts the day you meet them.\n\nThe six ways: phone, face to face, letter, email, SMS and video.\n\n## Cold leads (give value, ask for nothing)\n\n- Day 1, phone: thank them for their time.\n- Day 2, letter: the Introduction letter.\n- Day 4, video: this week's market update.\n- Day 7, SMS: one handy fact about their suburb.\n- Day 9, letter: the Testimonial letter, with numbers to call.\n- Day 11, email: how to pick the right agent.\n- Day 17, video: where the fee goes, step by step.\n- Day 25, email: a client review, in the client's own words.\n- Day 28, phone: tell them about a recent sale nearby.\n\n## Warm leads (give value first, then ask)\n\n- Day 1, phone: ask what has them thinking about selling.\n- Day 2, letter: the Introduction letter.\n- Day 4, video: a market update made just for them.\n- Day 7, phone: answer any questions they have.\n- Day 11, email: your selling process on one page.\n- Day 14, letter: the Presentation letter, with your partners.\n- Day 17, video: answer the worry they raised.\n- Day 21, phone: offer to walk through the home.\n- Day 28, phone: ask for the appraisal.\n\n## Hot leads (ask for the next step)\n\n- Day 1, phone: book the appraisal.\n- Day 4, SMS: confirm the time.\n- Day 7, video: walk them through how appraisal day runs.\n- Day 11, email: one recent sale and what made it work.\n- Day 14, letter: the Presentation letter, with your partners.\n- Day 15, phone: ask for the listing.\n- Day 18, letter: the Legal letter, with your conveyancers.\n- Day 21, letter: the Broker letter.\n- Day 24, letter: the Storage letter.\n- Day 25, phone: ask who else they know is thinking of selling.\n- Day 28, letter: a handwritten thank-you.\n\n> Treat these as a starting point. Move the days and swap the modes to suit your market. Just keep the order: value goes out first, and the asks come last.\n\n- Who helps me run the plans: [name]\n- Loaded into the CRM by: [name, date]\n\n## How it fits 300 / 30 / 3 (office guidance)\n\n- Day 0 is the day you meet them: at the door, at an open home, on a call. Tag the group on the Quick Appraisal form and start the plan the same day.\n- A geo-data contact with no stated need is usually a Cold lead. An appraisal with a 2 to 12 month timeframe is usually Warm. A 1 to 2 month appraisal, or anyone who asked you for an appraisal, is Hot.\n- Moving people up is the job. A Cold lead who replies becomes Warm. A Warm lead who says yes to the day 28 appraisal becomes Hot and starts the Hot plan the next day.\n- The Hot plan is your standard checklist after every appraisal with a 1 to 2 month timeframe, and every appraisal the owner asked for. If the appraisal is already done, the visit is day 0, and the day 7 video becomes the fee video. Day 15 is where the 3 listings come from."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "lost-listing-case-study",
  "title": "The Lost Listing Review",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Complete in week 6 for every unsuccessful appraisal and missed farm sale so far, and again at graduation; check the contact rhythm first.",
  "body": "## Part A: lost listings in your farm (Pipeline Rhythm)\n\nOne row for each listing you lost in the farm: an appraisal that listed with another agent, or a farm sale where the owner was already in our database.\n\n- Who or where: [address or name]\n- Month it listed: [month]\n- Time from meeting them to listing: [1 to 2 months / 2 to 12 months]\n- Why we lost it: [what you think happened]\n\nThe contact rhythm: 1 to 2 months, every week. 2 to 12 months, every month.\n\nFor every row, ask: did they hear from us at that rhythm? If a 1 to 2 month seller went three weeks without hearing from you, you have found the reason.\n\n## Part B: your last three lost listings (The 30-Day Trust Plan)\n\n> List them. Circle the lead group each was in at your first meeting. Then tick the modes you used every time, not just once.\n\n- 1. Address or name: [ ]. Group: C / W / H. Modes kept up: phone / face to face / letter / email / SMS / video. Why I think I lost it: [ ]\n- 2. Address or name: [ ]. Group: C / W / H. Modes kept up: phone / face to face / letter / email / SMS / video. Why I think I lost it: [ ]\n- 3. Address or name: [ ]. Group: C / W / H. Modes kept up: phone / face to face / letter / email / SMS / video. Why I think I lost it: [ ]\n\nC = Cold, W = Warm, H = Hot.\n\nWhat I was not doing:\n\n- 1. [ ]\n- 2. [ ]\n- 3. [ ]\n\n## Office guidance\n\n- For a new associate, lost listings include the ones that never reached an appraisal: the geo-data contact who said \"maybe next year\" in week 2 and listed with someone else in week 9. That is a missed sale, and it counts.\n- The pattern is nearly always the same: one mode only (phone and nothing else), or contact that stopped after the appraisal. Part B makes it visible.\n- Damian or Luke will take you through the four common triggers for a lost listing at your week 6 Checkpoint Conversation. Write them here then, rather than guessing: 1. [ ] 2. [ ] 3. [ ] 4. [ ]\n- Bring both parts to your week 6 and week 12 Checkpoint Conversations. Your \"what I was not doing\" lines become next month's drills."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "missed-sales-audit",
  "title": "The Missed Sales Audit (last 3 months)",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Do it in week 1 to see where your farm's sales are going, and again in week 12; a missed sale is any sale where the owner was in our database but listed elsewhere.",
  "body": "## Why every contact counts: the arithmetic\n\n- Start with 1,000 owners in a farm.\n- About 5% of homes change hands in a typical year (the program's benchmark), so that is 50 sales.\n- At $10,000 commission each, those 50 sales are worth $500,000.\n- Divide by 1,000 owners and each contact is worth roughly $500 a year to whoever holds the relationship.\n\n## Your farm\n\n- Homes in my farm: [number]\n- Sales a year at 5%: [homes x 0.05]\n- Office average commission per sale: $[ask Damian or Luke]\n- Commission available in the farm each year: [sales x commission]\n- Contacts I hold today: [number]\n- 300 contacts at about $500 each: roughly $150,000 a year of future income sitting in your CRM (300 x $500, using the benchmark assumptions)\n\n## Sales in your farm, last 3 months\n\nOne row per farm sale in the last three months, by any agency.\n\n- 1. Address: [ ]. Agency: [ ]. Sold: [date]. In our database? Yes / No. Contact from us in the last 12 months? Yes / No. What we could have done: [ ]\n- 2. Address: [ ]. Agency: [ ]. Sold: [date]. In our database? Yes / No. Contact from us in the last 12 months? Yes / No. What we could have done: [ ]\n- 3. Address: [ ]. Agency: [ ]. Sold: [date]. In our database? Yes / No. Contact from us in the last 12 months? Yes / No. What we could have done: [ ]\n- 4. Address: [ ]. Agency: [ ]. Sold: [date]. In our database? Yes / No. Contact from us in the last 12 months? Yes / No. What we could have done: [ ]\n\n## Totals\n\n- Farm sales in the last 3 months: [n]\n- Sold by Ray White Mildura: [n]\n- In our database but listed elsewhere: [n] (these are your missed sales)\n- Not in any database: [n] (geo data you do not have yet)\n\n## What to do with it\n\n- Every missed sale gets a Just Sold door knock and the Fifty Neighbours calls around it. The sellers have moved on; their neighbours have not.\n- Every \"not in any database\" address tells you which streets go first on the Street Appraisal Days calendar.\n- Redo this in week 12. The \"in our database but listed elsewhere\" number should be zero."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "capacity-tiers",
  "title": "Contact Tiers: Bronze, Silver, Gold, Platinum",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Tier every contact in week 6 and again in week 12, then book the annual appraisal for Silver and above; only promise the rhythm you can actually keep.",
  "body": "Each contact gets a set number of touches a year, by tier. Personal touches are one-to-one: calls, check-ins, cards and appraisals. Broadcast touches go to the whole tier and run from the CRM.\n\n## Bronze\n\n- Personal: 2 calls a year, 2 One-Line Check-ins\n- Broadcast: 2 newsletters a year, plus the weekly email update (the Monday Market Note) while they are in the active pipeline\n\n## Silver\n\n- Personal: an annual appraisal, 4 calls a year, 2 One-Line Check-ins, a monthly SMS, 1 handwritten card (thank you or anniversary)\n- Broadcast: 4 newsletters a year, the weekly email update, 1 printed newsletter\n\n## Gold\n\n- Personal: an annual appraisal, 4 calls a year, 4 One-Line Check-ins, a monthly SMS, a monthly video update, 2 handwritten cards (thank you or anniversary), a Christmas gift\n- Broadcast: 6 newsletters a year, the weekly email update, 2 printed newsletters\n\n## Platinum\n\n- Personal: an annual appraisal, 4 calls a year, 6 One-Line Check-ins, a monthly video update, a client movie night each quarter, 2 handwritten cards (thank you or anniversary), a Christmas gift\n- Broadcast: 12 newsletters a year, the weekly email update, a community newsletter every second month\n\n## Who goes where (office guidance)\n\n- Bronze: geo-data contact, no stated need, no appraisal yet.\n- Silver: anyone you have appraised who is not Gold, or anyone who has told you they may sell in the next 12 months.\n- Gold: appraised with a 1 to 2 month timeframe, or a Hot lead; also past office clients you have been handed.\n- Platinum: your own past clients and anyone who has referred you a lead. You will earn these.\n\n## Your worksheet\n\n- Contacts in Bronze: [n]. Silver: [n]. Gold: [n]. Platinum: [n].\n- Personal touches each contact gets in a year, counted from the tiers above: Bronze 4, Silver about 20, Gold about 36, Platinum about 26 plus the quarterly movie nights.\n- 300 Bronze contacts means 1,200 personal touches a year: 600 One-Line Check-ins, which you send from your own mobile in your admin blocks, and 600 calls, which are your call sessions. The newsletters come on top and run from the CRM, plus the Monday Market Note for anyone on your pipeline board.\n- Can you keep it up? Put people in the tier you can actually service, not the one you wish you could. Promise less and deliver all of it.\n- Personal beats broadcast. A call, a card or an appraisal builds trust; a newsletter on its own rarely does. Do the personal touches yourself and let the CRM send the broadcast ones."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "ai-message-prompt",
  "title": "AI message prompt (Claude or ChatGPT)",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Run it once per lead group in week 7 to draft your 12 messages (four emotions for each of three groups), then read every draft aloud and check every number before it goes.",
  "body": "## Paste this into Claude or ChatGPT\n\n> You are helping me write messages to listing leads for my real estate business.\n> ABOUT ME: [my name, my office, the suburbs I work, my recent results].\n> THE LEAD: [a cold lead who does not trust me yet / a warm lead who knows me / a hot lead who is ready to act].\n> Write four messages for this lead. Each one speaks to a different emotion: their fears, proof, education, and who I am as a person.\n> Cold lead: give value only and ask for nothing. Warm lead: give value first, then make a gentle ask. Hot lead: ask clearly for the next step.\n> For every message I need two things: four talking points for a short video, and an email of 120 words or fewer with a subject line.\n> Where I give you numbers, use them. If a number is missing, put a [bracket] where it goes and I will add it. Never invent a statistic.\n> Sound like me talking. Keep it short and plain. No marketing words, and nothing that reads like a pitch.\n> Use Australian English.\n\n## Then ask for these\n\n- Give me this as four video bullet points.\n- Cut the email down and make it warmer.\n- Turn it into an SMS of 300 characters or fewer.\n- Work in my figures: [recent sales, median price, open home attendance].\n\n## The rules\n\n- AI writes the draft. You read it aloud before it goes anywhere.\n- Every number gets checked against your own figures before it goes out.\n- Sent beats perfect. Send the first one today.\n\nThe first message I will build: [group, emotion, lead's first name]\n\n## Office guidance\n\n- Run it three times, once per group, for your 12 messages. Save them as CRM templates so the trust plans can send them.\n- \"My office\" is Ray White Mildura and \"the suburbs I work\" are your farm, for example Mildura, Irymple, Red Cliffs or Merbein. \"My recent results\" are the office's until you have your own; say so in the brackets.\n- Your numbers come from the office: weekly sales, open home attendance, days on market. Ask Damian or Luke. Never let the tool invent one, and never send a draft with a [bracket] still in it.\n- Never paste a client's full name, address, phone or email into the tool. First name only; add the details back in the CRM.\n- Read it aloud. If it does not sound like you at the door, change it until it does."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "pre-visit-checklist",
  "title": "Pre-visit checklist (before every door, drop or appraisal)",
  "kind": "checklist",
  "when": "Run it the night before and in the car before every market-report drop, kerbside appraisal or Doorstep Appraisal.",
  "body": "Office guidance, built from the Doorstep Script, the Farm Introduction script and the appraisal forms.\n\n## The night before\n\n- The street or address is on this week's Street Appraisal Days calendar, and the letter landed 3 to 5 days ago.\n- Three or four comparable sales from the last six months, written down, with a conservative range for each home you expect to see.\n- A printed market report for every door, plus spares. Business cards.\n- Printed Quick Appraisal forms in the folder.\n- CRM check: is this owner already in the system? Any notes from a call? A settlement date?\n- Booked appointments confirmed and the 30-minutes-before SMS scheduled.\n\n## In the car\n\n- Read the Doorstep Script once, out loud. The beats: hand over the report, offer two options (kerbside or a quick walk through), mention your next appointment so they know it will be short, give a conservative range, plant the idea of a full appraisal, and promise to stay in touch.\n- Choose the first door and your direction. Start at the end of the street with the most comparable sales.\n- Phone on silent, notepad in your pocket, forms in the folder.\n\n## At the door\n\n- Your name, Ray White Mildura and the reason you are there, inside ten seconds.\n- Respect \"do not knock\" and \"no canvassing\" signs. Leave the report in the letterbox and move on.\n- Two options, their choice: kerbside now, or a quick walk through. Never push the walk through.\n- Give a range, never a single figure, and never promise a price. It is an appraisal, not a valuation.\n- Ask whether there is anything property related you can help with in the next 12 months, and collect name, address, phone and email.\n- Anything that needs a follow-up gets two times offered on the spot.\n\n## Before you drive off (2 minutes)\n\n- Quick Appraisal form done: action plan, category, precinct, lead group.\n- Geo data added or confirmed in the CRM, with the settlement date if they told you.\n- Appraisal logged in the app, with the detail sheet filled in: address, 1 to 2 months or 2 to 12 months, Cold / Warm / Hot, action plan. That puts it on your pipeline with the next touch date. Not selling? Tap \"Skip, just count it\" instead.\n- In the CRM: 2-hours-after SMS set, 12-month letter and anniversary SMS dates set, Day 2 Introduction letter queued for Cold and Warm leads, and the right 30-day trust plan started.\n\n## Compliance (office guidance)\n\n- We give market appraisals, not valuations. Only a licensed valuer gives a valuation.\n- Keep the exclusive-agency note on every letter that has one (Street Appraisal, seven-year, both 12-month letters, Just Listed, Just Sold). If another agent holds a current exclusive agency on a property, we do not interfere.\n- Anyone who asks not to be contacted is marked \"do not contact\" in the CRM that day, and we honour it.\n- Unsure about anything legal in Victoria? Ask Damian or Luke before, not after."
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "geo-data-definition",
  "title": "What counts as one piece of geo data",
  "kind": "checklist",
  "when": "Check before you log a contact; it counts toward your 300 only if every line in the first list is true.",
  "body": "Office guidance. The Farm Introduction script collects a name, an address and a phone number; the letters also need an email and a settlement date. This is the office's definition for the 300.\n\n## One piece of geo data is\n\n- A NEW contact record. The person was not already in the Ray White Mildura CRM. Search by address and by surname before you add them.\n- The owner (or the decision maker) of a property inside your farm. Not a tenant, and not a tradie on site.\n- Their name. At least a first name and surname you could put on a letter.\n- A way to reach them: a mobile or landline, or an email address. Both is better.\n- The property address in the farm, matched to the record.\n- Entered in the CRM the same day, with the source (door, call session, open home, OFI callback, referral) and the date.\n\n## Capture where you can (it makes the letters work)\n\n- Email, if you got the phone number. Phone, if you got the email.\n- Settlement date, or roughly when they bought. The seven-year letter runs on it.\n- Precinct or street group, for the Street Appraisal Days calendar.\n- Lead group: Cold (no stated need), Warm (thinking about it, 2 to 12 months), Hot (1 to 2 months, or asked for an appraisal).\n- Their answer to \"anything property related in the next 12 months?\", in their own words, in the notes.\n- Anything personal that helps the next call: the kids' footy, the new deck, the dog's name.\n\n## What does not count toward the 300\n\n- A name with no phone and no email. Log it, but it is a lead to go back for, not geo data.\n- An existing contact you updated. Good work, but not new data.\n- A buyer or renter who does not own in the farm. Add them as a buyer; they do not count.\n- A property outside the farm. Add it to the database; it does not count.\n- Anyone who asked not to be contacted. Mark the address \"do not contact\" and move on.\n\n## How you collect it (the Farm Introduction script)\n\n- The beats, in order. Introduce yourself and Ray White Mildura. Give the reason: the free market report the neighbours have been getting. Offer to send them one too if you can take their details. Collect name, address and phone number. Look for an opening: anything property related in the next 12 months? Close with the next step: thank them, and tell them the report is on its way.\n- Then send what you promised within a day. That is the first touch, and it makes the next call easy. Telling them what you will send is also how they agree to hear from you.\n\n## The maths\n\n- 300 over 12 weeks is about 25 a week, or 5 a working day. Two green call sessions plus one street of doors gets you there.\n- The program's benchmark: each farm contact is worth about $500 a year (1,000 contacts x 5% selling x $10,000 commission). Your Mildura figure uses the office's average commission; work it out with Damian or Luke."
});

