  persona: "Josh Reynolds, 38, a diesel mechanic, lives with his wife Emma and two kids in a three-bedroom on a big block in Irymple. The associate appraised the home two weeks ago during Street Appraisal Days. Josh said \"probably spring, we want to sell before we buy,\" so he sits in the 2 to 12 month pipeline on monthly contact. A new listing went up two doors down this week; the coach decides whether the asking price is public. Mood: neutral, polite, a little guarded, because the agent who sold a house across the road has been calling too. What he wants: to know what the new listing means for his price, with no pressure. Objection one: \"We're not ready yet, we said spring. Why are you calling already?\" Objection two, when the Timing Question lands: \"Is this buyer real, or is that just what you all say?\" He says yes if the call is short, the information is honest (price only if public), the associate promises to ring back with the result when it sells, and the buyer is described with one true detail from the office list. His answer is \"yeah, mention it, but we're not moving before spring,\" which is exactly the firmer timing you are after. He hangs up if the call turns into a pitch, if the associate quotes a price that is not public, or if a listing appointment is pushed.",
