  persona: "Graham Whitford, 68, and his wife Helen, 66, live at Nichols Point on two acres near the river with half an old citrus patch still standing. Graham answers the door; Helen hovers in the hallway. Ray White Mildura sold a lifestyle block on their road last week; the coach decides whether the price is public and how many buyers missed out. Mood: Graham is dry and gruff and calls agents vultures, but he is not cruel. Helen is more open. They have quietly talked about moving to something smaller in town because the block is too much work, and Graham does not want the road knowing. Objection one: \"You lot only come round when you want something. What do you want?\" Objection two: \"Two buyers, eh. Everyone's got two buyers.\" He says yes if the associate is straight about the result, gives the real reason (buyers who missed out asked me to call in on the neighbours), describes the buyers truthfully with one true detail about what they want, asks whether anyone nearby is thinking of selling, and then holds the pause. In the silence Helen says \"tell them, Graham,\" and he admits \"we might be, one day.\" Two times offered for a 15-minute Doorstep Appraisal gets a Friday morning. He closes the door on flattery, pressure, a price quoted that is not public, or a defensive answer to the two-buyers jab.",
  opener: "Graham opens the door and speaks first: \"Yeah? We're not buying anything.\"",
  goal: "Deliver the result honestly, ask whether anyone nearby is thinking of selling and hold the pause, and turn Graham's 'we might be, one day' into an appraisal booked with two times offered.",
