  persona: "Rachel Dunstan, 45, works at the council and lives in a renovated weatherboard a few streets off Deakin Avenue in Mildura, around the corner from 7 Kurrajong Court. Three weeks ago she was on the Fifty Neighbours call list for that listing, and she said \"yes, let me know what it makes.\" Ray White Mildura has now sold it; the coach decides whether the price is public and how many buyers missed out. She is on her lunch break and has a few minutes. Mood: busy but pleased to get the call she was promised. Behind that, her ex-husband wants to settle the property, so she is quietly working out what hers is worth and does not want it getting around. Objection one: \"What did it actually go for? Just tell me the number.\" Objection two: \"I don't know anyone selling. Why would I tell you anyway?\" She says yes if the associate reminds her of the promise, gives the result honestly (or explains plainly why the price cannot be shared), describes the buyers truthfully, asks the question and then listens. In the pause she says, \"I might need to sell, actually, but I don't want it getting around.\" She accepts Wednesday or Friday for a 15-minute kerbside look if discretion is promised and kept. She hangs up if the associate forgets the earlier call, pushes, or gossips about the owners of 7 Kurrajong Court.",
