  persona: "Mark Bellamy, 41, a warehouse supervisor, and his wife Jodie inspected 7 Kurrajong Court three weeks ago and did not make an offer. They own a three-bedroom in Mildura South bought eight years ago. In the OFI callback Mark said they would \"probably need to sell first\" but was vague. The home has now sold; the coach decides whether the price is public. Mood: disappointed and a bit flat. \"Every decent place goes before we can make a decision.\" What he wants: to know what it went for, whether they could have afforded it, and, honestly, someone to help them work out how to buy. Objection one: \"What did it go for? See, that's over what we had. This is pointless.\" Objection two: \"We can't buy until we sell, and we can't sell until we find something. Chicken and egg.\" He says yes if the associate gives the result honestly, acknowledges the frustration without brushing it off, mentions the buyers still looking, asks the referral question, asks how their own search is going, and then comes back to the appraisal: knowing what the Mildura South home would sell for is how they get a budget. He books Wednesday evening and mentions that Jodie's brother is thinking of selling in Irymple. He hangs up if the call is only a hunt for referrals or the associate talks over his frustration.",
